// Generated by scripts/build-academy-data.mjs from the Season 1 production sources. Do not edit by hand.
export const ACADEMY_CONTENT = Object.freeze({"locale":"en","lessons":[{"ep":1,"title":"One bitcoin costs that much. Can I still buy some?","goal":"Tell the number of coins apart from the amount you spend, and know bitcoin can be bought in fractions.","task":"In the practice panel, convert a spend of 100 into a coin amount. No real order is created.","answer":"0.002 BTC (fees not deducted).","takeaways":["The price of one bitcoin is not the price of getting started.","Bitcoin divides into decimals: spend 100 at 50,000 and you hold 0.002 BTC.","Each platform sets its own minimum order and fees, so check both first."],"quiz":{"q":"One bitcoin is 50,000 USDT. You spend 100 USDT, ignoring fees. What do you get?","options":["Nothing, you need a whole coin","0.002 BTC","0.02 BTC"],"answer":1,"why":"100 ÷ 50,000 = 0.002. You can hold a fraction of one bitcoin."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"One coin costs that much? I can't even afford a ticket in."},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"That's the price of one whole coin. It's not what you have to pay each time. Bitcoin is a digital asset, and you can hold it in decimal amounts. You usually don't buy a whole one."},{"t":25.25,"who":"narrator","text":"One practice sum. Say one bitcoin is 50,000 USDT and you spend 100. Ignoring fees, that's 0.002 bitcoin. This is not today's market price."},{"t":45.25,"who":"sam","text":"And when the price moves?"},{"t":47,"who":"nexubo","text":"The number of coins can stay the same, but their value goes up and down. Buying a little means risking less. It doesn't mean no risk."},{"t":62.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"You can split it, but not every platform accepts any tiny order. Check the minimum amount and the fees. While you learn, use virtual funds. No deposit needed."},{"t":75.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Do you have to buy a whole bitcoin? No. Remember: the price of one coin is not the price of getting started."},{"t":86.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Most trades are priced in USDT. But is USDT really a dollar? Next episode."},{"t":92.55,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":2,"title":"Is USDT a dollar? Why is it everywhere when you buy crypto?","goal":"Understand what a stablecoin is for, what it aims to track and its risks, and know you don't always need USDT first.","task":"Sort 'dollar bank deposit', 'USDT' and 'BTC' into the right categories.","answer":"USDT is a stablecoin, a type of crypto asset. It is not a dollar bank deposit.","takeaways":["USDT is a stablecoin designed to stay close to one dollar, not a bank deposit.","Exchanges use it to price and swap assets, so a sale often ends in USDT.","\"Stable\" is a design goal: the price can drift and redemption has conditions."],"quiz":{"q":"Which statement about USDT is true?","options":["It is the dollars in your bank account","It is a stablecoin, a type of crypto asset","It can never move away from one dollar"],"answer":1,"why":"USDT aims to track the dollar, but it is a crypto asset with its own issuer and platform risks."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"It's called Tether, so it's basically dollars in the bank, right?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"No. USDT, or Tether, is a stablecoin. It's designed to stay close to one dollar. But it isn't the dollars in your bank account, and it isn't risk-free cash."},{"t":25.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Exchanges often use it to price and swap assets. You might buy bitcoin with USDT, and when you sell, you may get USDT back, not a bank transfer."},{"t":45.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"'Stable' is a design goal, not a guarantee. The price can drift, and issuing and redeeming come with conditions. If you hold it, learn about the issuer and the platform risks too."},{"t":62.25,"who":"sam","text":"So I have to buy USDT before bitcoin?"},{"t":65.23,"who":"nexubo","text":"Not always. Which currencies and trading pairs you can use depends on your region and your platform."},{"t":75.25,"who":"narrator","text":"What's wrong with this sentence? A stablecoin is not a promise to protect your money. Know what it's for, and know it can be unstable."},{"t":86.25,"who":"narrator","text":"If your phone breaks tonight, are your coins gone too? Next: where your coins actually live."},{"t":92.15,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":3,"title":"Where are my coins? Exchange vs wallet","goal":"Tell a custodial exchange account from a self-custody wallet, and understand who controls access and who handles recovery.","task":"Match two scenarios: an exchange login account, and a standard recovery-phrase self-custody wallet.","answer":"They differ in who has control and how recovery works. Self-custody does not mean automatically safe.","takeaways":["On an exchange account, the platform mainly manages the assets and keys.","In a self-custody wallet you control the credentials, and the recovery duty is yours.","Ask who controls the permission to move the assets before you choose."],"quiz":{"q":"You lose the recovery phrase of a standard self-custody wallet and have no other recovery method. What happens?","options":["Support can reset it for you","Access may be lost for good","The exchange restores it automatically"],"answer":1,"why":"With self-custody you hold the keys. Without the phrase or another valid method, nobody can recover it for you."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"My phone broke. Are my coins smashed too?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Your coins aren't inside your phone. A wallet lets you see assets on the blockchain and uses keys to approve actions. Whether you can recover depends on the wallet type and the recovery options you kept."},{"t":32.25,"who":"narrator","text":"With a custodial exchange account, the platform mainly manages the assets and keys, and you act through your account. With a self-custody wallet, you control the credentials. Convenience and responsibility are split differently."},{"t":60.25,"who":"sam","text":"Forget your password on either one, and you just call support?"},{"t":63.86,"who":"nexubo","text":"Don't think of it that way. With a standard recovery-phrase wallet, if you lose the phrase and have no other valid recovery method, support can't get it back for you."},{"t":84.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Keeping assets on a platform carries platform risk. Holding them yourself can go wrong through a leaked key or a bad approval. Today, just understand the difference. You don't need to move everything right now."},{"t":103.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Who controls the permission to move the assets? Answer that first. Then decide which account or wallet you need."},{"t":116.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Two doors look the same. One is fake. Next episode: how to spot the real one."},{"t":121.75,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":4,"title":"Find the real door first: don't open an account on a fake platform","goal":"Check region eligibility, the official entry and the fees first; then sign up and turn on security settings.","task":"In a mock platform profile, find the unsupported region, the abnormal entry link and the undisclosed fees.","answer":"If any key eligibility or entry problem is unresolved, do not continue.","takeaways":["Check that the platform serves your real country before anything else.","Sign up only through the confirmed official entry, with your own email or phone.","Right after sign-up, turn on security features and save your recovery options."],"quiz":{"q":"What should you confirm first, before signing up?","options":["The size of the welcome reward","That the platform serves your country and the entry is official","How many followers the platform has"],"answer":1,"why":"Eligibility and the official entry come first. A reward should never decide where you open an account."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"This one has a huge reward. I'll just go in here?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Don't let a reward decide. First check: is this allowed where you live? Does the platform accept your real country of residence? Which company provides the service? Then look at fees, deposits and withdrawal rules."},{"t":32.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Once you've confirmed the official entry, sign up with your own email or phone number, set a unique password, and complete the checks the page asks for. Never buy someone else's account, and never fake your identity or region."},{"t":60.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Signing up isn't the last step. Turn on the security features the platform supports, check your logged-in devices, and save your recovery options. And never send a one-time code to a so-called support agent."},{"t":84.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Our site can help you look things up, but a partnership is not a safety guarantee. If the facts aren't confirmed, don't start the sign-up."},{"t":103.25,"who":"sam","text":"Stop. Confirm eligibility and the rules first."},{"t":107.02,"who":"nexubo","text":"Right. Find the real door, then open the account."},{"t":116.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Want a door you can trust? NexuAlpha lists each exchange's verified official link. Our OKX partner link goes straight to the official site, and code nexu888 adds a fee rebate."},{"t":127.35,"who":"narrator","text":"Do it now: turn on two-factor login. Then come back. Next, someone in a chat offers to 'do your KYC for you'."},{"t":134.45,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":5,"title":"How does KYC work, and who should get your ID?","goal":"Understand the general purpose and flow of identity verification, and submit only through a verified official channel.","task":"Put the verification steps in order using fictional details. Uploading a real ID to the course is not allowed.","answer":"Verify the entry → submit your real details as your region requires → wait and check the result.","takeaways":["KYC confirms you are the account owner and helps the platform meet the rules.","Submit your ID only inside the official verification flow, never in a chat.","If it fails, read the reason, fix it and try again with your real identity."],"quiz":{"q":"Who should receive your ID?","options":["A helpful \"agent\" in a chat group","Only the verified official verification flow","The course chat, so others can check it"],"answer":1,"why":"Only the official flow. NexuAlpha never asks for your ID, and neither should a stranger."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"It wants my ID. And this guy says he can do it for me?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Identity checks are usually there to confirm you are the account owner and to meet the rules. Make sure it's the official flow first. Don't hand your ID or codes to an 'agent' in a chat group."},{"t":32.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Usually you enter your real details, upload a supported valid ID, then follow the prompts for a photo or face check. Whether you need proof of address, and which IDs are accepted, depends on your region and platform."},{"t":60.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"If it fails, read the reason first. Is the ID expired? Is there glare? Does the name match? Fix it as instructed. Never try again with a fake identity."},{"t":84.25,"who":"sam","text":"Once I'm done, can I trade right away?"},{"t":86.78,"who":"nexubo","text":"Not guaranteed. The result, the products you can use and whether you can deposit all depend on the official notice. And this academy never collects your real ID."},{"t":103.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Who gets your ID? Only the verified official flow. Not us, and not a stranger."},{"t":116.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Ready to verify? Start only on the official site. Our OKX partner link takes you straight there, with code nexu888 for a fee rebate. We never ask for your ID."},{"t":127.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Deposit, buy, transfer: three buttons that all look the same. Next: which one to press."},{"t":132.85,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":6,"title":"First deposit: where does the money come from, and where does it go?","goal":"Tell apart a fiat deposit, buying crypto, an on-chain deposit and an internal transfer.","task":"Match three sources of funds to: fiat deposit, on-chain deposit and internal transfer.","answer":"Bank money and on-chain token addresses can't be mixed. Check each channel separately.","takeaways":["A fiat deposit, buying crypto, an on-chain deposit and an internal transfer are different routes.","On-chain deposits need a supported coin and network; internal transfers stay on the platform.","Never pay a stranger off-platform; check where the balance will land first."],"quiz":{"q":"You move USDT from your funding account to your trading account on the same platform. What is that?","options":["An on-chain deposit","An internal transfer","A fiat deposit"],"answer":1,"why":"It only moves balance between your own accounts. It is usually not an on-chain transfer."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"These all look like putting money in. Which one do I press?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Start with what you're holding. Money in your bank, coins you already have somewhere else, or a balance in another account on the same platform. Each one takes a different route."},{"t":32.25,"who":"narrator","text":"A fiat deposit usually adds a cash balance to your account. Buying crypto directly means you pay and receive a crypto asset. Some platforms support bank or card channels, some don't, and fees and limits differ."},{"t":60.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"If you already hold crypto, you can deposit it using a supported coin and network. An internal transfer just moves balance between your accounts. It's usually not an on-chain transfer. Check where the balance will land first."},{"t":84.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Some platforms also offer trading between users. That's not another name for a bank transfer. Never pay off-platform in private, never use funds of unknown origin or third-party payments. If it's unclear, don't do it yet."},{"t":103.25,"who":"sam","text":"No. They're different channels."},{"t":105.59,"who":"nexubo","text":"Right. Look at the asset first, then the route, and finally check what actually arrived."},{"t":116.25,"who":"narrator","text":"BTC/USDT: does that slash mean you buy two coins? Next: your first spot trade."},{"t":122.45,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":7,"title":"Your first spot trade: from 100 to 0.002 BTC","goal":"Complete a practice spot buy with a clear amount, quantity and fee.","task":"With a virtual balance of 1,000, buy 0.002 BTC. Check the remaining USDT balance of 899.90 and the trade record.","answer":"Amount 100, fee 0.10, total spent 100.10; 899.90 left.","takeaways":["BTC/USDT means bitcoin priced in USDT: you pay USDT and receive bitcoin.","Check whether the order box wants an amount of money or a number of coins.","After you buy, check the trade record: fill status, average price and fee."],"quiz":{"q":"You buy 0.002 BTC at 50,000 with a 0.1% fee taken separately in USDT. What is the total spent?","options":["100","100.10","110"],"answer":1,"why":"100 for the coins plus a 0.10 fee makes 100.10."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"This slash. Do I have to buy both coins?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"No. It means bitcoin is priced in USDT. When you buy, you pay USDT and get bitcoin. When you sell, it goes the other way."},{"t":35.25,"who":"narrator","text":"We practise with a virtual account. Make sure it's spot, not futures. Pick the pair, choose Buy, then enter the amount. Before you submit, check whether the box wants an amount of money or a number of coins."},{"t":67.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Say the fill price is 50,000 and you spend 100. You get 0.002 bitcoin. Now assume a 0.1% fee taken separately from your USDT. You spend 100.10 in total. Real rules depend on the platform."},{"t":95.25,"who":"sam","text":"So once I tap Buy, I definitely have the coins?"},{"t":99.07,"who":"nexubo","text":"Check the trade history too. Not filled, partly filled and fully filled are different. Check the real average price and the fee as well."},{"t":116.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Your first practice isn't about guessing up or down. It's about checking these five things. And when you're done, it's fine to not make a real trade yet."},{"t":131.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"When you're ready for a real first trade, open Bitget through our partner link. Code nexu888 gets part of your trading fees back. Same five checks, every time."},{"t":140.95,"who":"narrator","text":"Say the price rises and you sell. So why is your bank balance still the same? Next episode."},{"t":146.85,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":8,"title":"I sold. Why isn't the money in my bank?","goal":"Tell apart selling an asset, getting a stablecoin or fiat balance, and an actual bank withdrawal.","task":"Complete a practice sell, then mark on the money-flow diagram which step is the bank withdrawal.","answer":"Selling to USDT is not a bank withdrawal. Net trading result in this example: 9.79.","takeaways":["Selling for USDT gives you a USDT balance, not money in your bank.","Selling and withdrawing to your bank are two separate steps.","A higher price is not your net gain: subtract every fee first."],"quiz":{"q":"You sold your bitcoin for USDT. Where is the money now?","options":["In your bank account","As a USDT balance on the platform","Already converted to your local currency"],"answer":1,"why":"You received USDT. Getting money to your bank is a separate withdrawal step."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"I definitely sold. So why hasn't my bank got the money?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Check which pair you sold into. If you sold for USDT, you got a USDT balance, not money in the bank. Selling and withdrawing to your bank are two separate steps."},{"t":35.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Same teaching example. You sell 0.002 bitcoin at 55,000. That's 110. Take off an assumed fee of 0.11, and 109.89 lands in your account."},{"t":67.25,"who":"sam","text":"So I made ten?"},{"t":68.76,"who":"nexubo","text":"Take off the costs. Here it's 9.79, before any currency conversion or withdrawal fees. A price going up isn't the same as your final net gain."},{"t":95.25,"who":"narrator","text":"If you need the money back in your bank, first check that your region supports it. Then convert or withdraw through the official route to an account in your own name. Check the name, the amount received, the fees and the processing conditions."},{"t":116.25,"who":"sam","text":"No."},{"t":116.98,"who":"nexubo","text":"Keep your trade and withdrawal records. If you're not sure what kind of balance it is, don't decide based on one 'success' message."},{"t":131.25,"who":"narrator","text":"One cheaper network. One wrong click. Your coins may never arrive. Next: the checks you never guess."},{"t":137.95,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":9,"title":"Before you transfer: don't guess any of these","goal":"Check the coin, network, address, memo and arrival conditions, and send a small test first.","task":"In a practice withdrawal form with deliberate errors, fix the network mismatch and the missing memo; then send a virtual small test.","answer":"Submit only when every key condition matches. There is no default 'auto-switch to the right network' protection.","takeaways":["Confirm the coin and the network the receiver supports before you send.","Copy the address from the receiving page, check all of it and add any memo or tag.","Send a small test first and confirm it was credited."],"quiz":{"q":"The receiving page asks for a memo. What do you do?","options":["Skip it, the address is enough","Fill in the memo exactly as shown","Use the memo from an old transfer"],"answer":1,"why":"If a memo or tag is required, the transfer may not be credited without it."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"This network is cheaper. Why not just switch?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"First confirm which asset and network the receiver supports. Then think about fees. If the two ends don't match, don't send just because it's cheaper. Even USDT can live on different networks."},{"t":39.25,"who":"narrator","text":"For now, think of the network as the route and the address as the destination. But two addresses can look alike and still not work on the same network. Always check that the receiving platform supports this chain and this asset."},{"t":75.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Copy the address from the receiving page, then check all of it. If a memo or tag is required, fill it in. Confirm the minimum deposit, the withdrawal fee and the amount that will arrive. Don't judge an address by its first and last few characters."},{"t":106.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Send a small test that meets the minimum, and confirm it was actually credited. For a bigger transfer, check everything again. Don't blindly reuse old records. For self-custody transfers, have the network fee ready too."},{"t":130.25,"who":"sam","text":"No."},{"t":131.16,"who":"nexubo","text":"One more check before sending beats looking for help after a mistake."},{"t":146.25,"who":"narrator","text":"It says 'success', but nothing arrived. Don't send it again. Next: where to look."},{"t":151.65,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":10,"title":"It says sent, but it never arrived. Where do you look?","goal":"Tell apart sender processing, on-chain confirmation and crediting by the receiver, and know the limited ways to fix things.","task":"Match three problem scenarios to: the sender, the on-chain status and the receiver.","answer":"Find the status, then use the matching official channel. If the cause is unknown, don't pay again.","takeaways":["Submitted, on-chain and credited are three different stations.","Use the transaction hash on a block explorer for the right network.","Share records with official support, never your recovery phrase, private key or codes."],"quiz":{"q":"Your transfer says \"sent\" but has not arrived. What is the first step?","options":["Send the same amount again","Find where it is stuck using the record and transaction hash","Pay someone who promises to recover it"],"answer":1,"why":"Find the station first, then contact the matching official channel. Do not keep sending."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"It didn't arrive. Should I just send a few more?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Don't send again. Submitted, on-chain and credited are not the same thing. Find which station it's stuck at, then decide who to contact."},{"t":32.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Keep the send record. If there's a transaction hash, check it on a proper block explorer for that network: status, destination address and amount. Don't mix in results from a different chain."},{"t":60.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"The receiving platform may still need confirmations or an internal review. If the coin, network, address or memo was wrong, stop sending and give the facts to official support."},{"t":84.25,"who":"narrator","text":"A completed on-chain transfer usually can't be undone like a normal action. Whether it can be recovered depends on technical and platform conditions. There's no guarantee. Don't believe anyone who says paying a fee will surely get it back."},{"t":103.25,"who":"sam","text":"Give the records they need. Never the recovery phrase, private key or codes."},{"t":107.92,"who":"nexubo","text":"Right. Find where it's stuck first. Don't keep sending."},{"t":116.25,"who":"narrator","text":"'Hi, I'm support. Send me your recovery phrase.' Next: what scammers really want."},{"t":122.45,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":11,"title":"Someone says he can make you money. First, see what he wants.","goal":"Recognise requests for secret credentials, off-platform payments, unusual approvals and guaranteed returns.","task":"Spot the dangerous requests in four fictional chats, and choose the 'stop and verify officially' path.","answer":"If anyone asks for your private key, recovery phrase, codes or an unclear approval, don't do it.","takeaways":["Your recovery phrase and private key are control keys, not support codes.","Guaranteed returns and pressure to pay are danger signs.","Never confirm a signature or approval you do not understand."],"quiz":{"q":"Which one is safe to share so you can receive a payment?","options":["Your public address","Your recovery phrase","A one-time code"],"answer":0,"why":"A public address is made for receiving. Secrets and codes give control away."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"He says he's support and he'll upgrade my wallet."},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"For a standard recovery-phrase wallet, the phrase is the control key, not a support code. Same for your private key. Never send them to anyone. Even our academy assistant should never ask for them."},{"t":32.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Two more danger signs: guaranteed returns, and pressure to pay. A screenshot of big profits proves nothing, and doesn't mean you could repeat it. If someone rushes you past the normal checks, stop."},{"t":60.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Connecting a wallet, signing a message and approving access to your assets are different things. If you don't understand a signature or approval, don't confirm it. No password prompt doesn't mean no risk."},{"t":84.25,"who":"narrator","text":"If you think something leaked, stop and use the official security channel. Act on what actually leaked. Never install remote-control software because a scammer told you to."},{"t":103.25,"who":"sam","text":"Sharing info to receive money isn't the same as handing over the keys."},{"t":106.72,"who":"nexubo","text":"Share no secrets. Approve nothing you don't understand. Don't be rushed into paying."},{"t":116.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Eleven episodes done. Is it time to deposit real money? The Season 1 finale has a surprising answer."},{"t":123.15,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":12,"title":"Season 1 finale: you can do it, and you can choose not to","goal":"Complete a practice spot trade and transfer checks on your own, and keep 'doing it right' separate from 'making a profit'.","task":"Complete, in order: a practice buy and sell, a fee check, rejecting a wrong-network transfer and spotting a scam. Key questions must all be correct.","answer":"You pass by following the correct flow or by actively stopping an unsuitable action. Profit is not scored.","takeaways":["A correct flow is the goal: entry and eligibility, spot, amount and fee, then the record.","Stopping an unclear transfer is a correct answer too.","Your certificate is a learning record, not a licence to invest."],"quiz":{"q":"A practice transfer shows the wrong network. What is the right move?","options":["Send it anyway and hope","Stop and do not send","Switch to a bigger amount"],"answer":1,"why":"When the network, fees or identity are unclear, stopping matters more than finishing the task."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"I've watched eleven episodes. Should I deposit now?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Learning how doesn't mean you have to invest. Today, with practice money only, we'll run one flow: check the entry and eligibility, find spot, confirm the amount and fee, then check the trade record."},{"t":39.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Even if the price falls in practice, you can still do everything right. We score whether you checked and understood, not whether you happened to pick a rise."},{"t":75.25,"who":"sam","text":"Something's wrong with this transfer. I'm not sending it."},{"t":78.5,"who":"nexubo","text":"That's a correct answer too. When the network, fees or identity aren't clear, stopping matters more than forcing the task through."},{"t":106.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Finally, say three things back. Where did the asset go? How much was actually taken? Who controls your account? If you can't explain one, go back to that episode and practise again."},{"t":130.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"What you've earned is a learning record, not a licence to invest. Keep learning, or don't trade for now. Both are fine. Understand first, then act."},{"t":146.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"When you do decide to start, compare exchanges side by side on NexuAlpha. Register through an official partner link with our code, get part of your fees back, and join our Telegram for new lessons."},{"t":157.55,"who":"narrator","text":"Season 2: the price on your screen isn't always the price you pay. Find out why."},{"t":163.05,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":13,"title":"Why isn't the price on screen my fill price?","goal":"Understand the order book, the bid-ask spread, market depth, fees and slippage.","task":"Match 0.002 BTC against two levels of virtual sell orders and work out the weighted average price.","answer":"Total 100.10, average 50,050. This is the trade amount before the teaching fee. If a 0.1% fee in USDT is also charged: fee 0.1001, total 100.2001 (don't round before calculating).","takeaways":["The last price is the most recent trade, not an offer for any size.","Larger orders can fill across several levels of the order book.","Slippage and the trading fee are separate costs: check the bill."],"quiz":{"q":"Half of your 0.002 BTC fills at 50,000 and the other half at 50,100. What is your average price?","options":["50,000","50,050","50,100"],"answer":1,"why":"Two equal halves average to 50,050. The fee is a separate cost on top."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"It was this price a second ago. Why is my bill different?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"The last price on screen is a record of the most recent trade. It's not an offer for unlimited size. How much people want to buy and sell right now is in the order book."},{"t":32.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Say you buy 0.002 bitcoin. The first level only has half. The other half fills at a higher price. Together, the average is 50,050, not 50,000."},{"t":60.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Slippage is the gap between the price you expected and the price you got. It isn't always against you. The trading fee is a separate cost. Don't lump them together under a 'free' slogan."},{"t":84.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Bigger orders, thinner depth or fast markets make it more likely the result drifts from what you expected. Check size and depth before you order, and the real average price after."},{"t":103.25,"who":"sam","text":"No."},{"t":104.04,"who":"nexubo","text":"You only know your fill when you see the bill. Don't just watch the biggest number."},{"t":116.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Fees are one cost you can actually lower. Compare fees on NexuAlpha, and register through an official partner link with our code to get part of your trading fees back. Lower cost, same risk."},{"t":126.75,"who":"narrator","text":"You placed a buy order and nothing happens. Is it broken? Next: market, limit and cancel."},{"t":132.45,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":14,"title":"Market, limit, cancel: what does each one solve?","goal":"Choose an order by 'fill first' or 'price limit first', and understand unfilled and partly filled orders.","task":"Place a virtual limit order, watch it partly fill, then cancel the remaining quantity.","answer":"Even at a suitable price, filling depends on available quantity and more. A cancel can't reverse what already filled.","takeaways":["A market order fills fast at the prices available now; the final price can move.","A limit order caps your price, but it may not fill.","Cancel only affects the unfilled part; what already filled stays."],"quiz":{"q":"Your limit order is half filled and you cancel it. What happens?","options":["Everything is reversed","Only the unfilled half is cancelled","The filled half turns into a market order"],"answer":1,"why":"A cancel cannot rewind a fill. It only stops the remaining quantity."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"I hit Buy. Why isn't it filling?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"A market order tries to fill quickly at the prices available now, but the final price can move. A limit order says 'buy no higher' or 'sell no lower' than a set price. It isn't guaranteed to fill."},{"t":32.25,"who":"narrator","text":"If you want to buy below the current price, you may have to wait. On the other hand, a limit order that already meets the other side's price can fill right away. Not every limit order has to wait in line."},{"t":60.25,"who":"sam","text":"So does cancelling undo what I already bought?"},{"t":62.82,"who":"nexubo","text":"No. Cancel only affects the part that hasn't filled. What already filled stays. It doesn't rewind."},{"t":84.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Read the status before you act. Whether you're the maker or the taker depends on how the trade actually filled. Choosing 'limit' doesn't automatically get you a certain fee."},{"t":103.25,"who":"sam","text":"No."},{"t":104.12,"who":"nexubo","text":"Choosing an order type is choosing how it executes. It's not making a wish."},{"t":116.25,"who":"narrator","text":"A green candle. Does it mean the next one goes up too? Next episode."},{"t":121.05,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":15,"title":"One candle: what four things does it tell you?","goal":"Read open, high, low, close and the time frame; don't treat colour or a single shape as a prediction.","task":"On a virtual candle, tap open, high, low and close, and confirm the chart time frame.","answer":"50,000 / 51,000 / 49,000 / 50,500; time frame five minutes.","takeaways":["One candle shows four prices for one period: open, high, low and close.","Read the time frame before the shape: five minutes is not a day.","Colours differ between apps, and one candle is a record, not a prediction."],"quiz":{"q":"A five-minute candle opens at 50,000, reaches 51,000, dips to 49,000 and closes at 50,500. What is the high?","options":["50,500","51,000","49,000"],"answer":1,"why":"The top of the upper wick is the highest price in that period: 51,000."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"Green means it keeps going up next, right?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"One candle records four prices for one period: open, high, low and close. This is made-up five-minute data. It's not a promise about the next five minutes."},{"t":32.25,"who":"narrator","text":"The body shows the gap between open and close. The wicks mark the highest and lowest prices in that period. A candle that hasn't finished yet keeps changing with new trades."},{"t":60.25,"who":"sam","text":"Why does the chart look different when I switch the time frame?"},{"t":63.75,"who":"nexubo","text":"Each candle now covers a different length of time. Read the time frame first, then the shape. Don't mistake five minutes for a day."},{"t":84.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Apps may use different colours for up and down, so read the legend and the numbers. Past moves can be informative, but one candle can't guarantee the next move."},{"t":103.25,"who":"sam","text":"High 51,000. Close 50,500."},{"t":108.91,"who":"nexubo","text":"Read the record first. Don't treat a record as a prophecy."},{"t":116.25,"who":"narrator","text":"'I have 1,000 and only want to risk 20. So I go all in?' Next: how much to buy."},{"t":122.35,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":16,"title":"Decide how much you could lose before you decide how much to buy","goal":"Understand that account balance, position size and planned risk are three different numbers.","task":"Given an entry, a stop distance and a teaching risk budget, work out the virtual position size.","answer":"400 USDT notional spot amount; 0.008 BTC; fees and execution differences are extra.","takeaways":["Account balance, position size and planned risk are three different numbers.","Decide your exit first, then size the position.","With a 5% stop and a 20 risk budget, the position is about 400."],"quiz":{"q":"Entry 50,000, planned exit 47,500, and you accept losing 20. About how big is the position?","options":["20","400","4,000"],"answer":1,"why":"The exit is 5% away, and 5% of 400 is 20. Costs come on top."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"I have 1,000 and only want to lose 20. So I just put it all in?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"What's in your account, how much you buy in this trade, and the most you plan to lose are three different things. Decide your exit first. Then you can size the position."},{"t":35.25,"who":"narrator","text":"One practice sum. Entry at 50,000, planned exit at 47,500. That's 5% away. If you plan to take a 20 price loss, ignoring costs, the position is about 400."},{"t":67.25,"who":"sam","text":"So 'lose at most 20' doesn't mean 'buy only 20'?"},{"t":70.55,"who":"nexubo","text":"Right. Here a 400 position falling 5% is the 20. These are teaching numbers only."},{"t":95.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Real markets have slippage, gaps, fees and failed orders, so planned risk is not a guaranteed maximum loss. Never borrow money for this course, and never practise with money you need to live on."},{"t":116.25,"who":"sam","text":"Twenty."},{"t":116.98,"who":"nexubo","text":"Work out the worst you can accept first. Then ask whether you need this trade at all."},{"t":131.25,"who":"narrator","text":"You set a stop-loss. Does that mean you can only lose that amount? Next: the truth about stops."},{"t":137.75,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":17,"title":"Take-profit and stop-loss are plans, not insurance","goal":"Understand trigger vs fill, and the different risks of stop-market and stop-limit orders.","task":"Compare the practice results of a stop-market and a stop-limit order in the same sharp drop.","answer":"The first may slip; the second may not fill. Neither is capital-protection insurance.","takeaways":["A stop-loss runs your exit plan; it is not insurance.","A stop-market exits fast but can slip in a fast market.","A stop-limit caps the price but may not fill at all."],"quiz":{"q":"In a sharp drop, what can happen to a stop-limit order?","options":["It always fills at the trigger price","It may not fill if the price races past","It turns into insurance"],"answer":1,"why":"The limit protects your price, so the order can stay unfilled when the market moves fast."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"My stop-loss is set, so I can only lose the amount I typed, right?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"A stop-loss runs your exit plan when a condition is met. It's not insurance. Being triggered doesn't mean you'll fill at that price, and it doesn't guarantee the loss stops exactly there."},{"t":32.25,"who":"narrator","text":"After the trigger, a stop-market order exits at market. It prioritises getting out fast, but the price can slip. In fast or thin markets, the gap can be bigger."},{"t":60.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"A stop-limit posts a limit order after the trigger. It caps the price you'll accept, but if the price races past, it may not sell at all. Neither one is always better. They're different trade-offs."},{"t":84.25,"who":"narrator","text":"After setting them, check the direction, quantity and order status. For futures, also check which price triggers it, so your stop isn't placed where liquidation could come first."},{"t":103.25,"who":"sam","text":"Not guaranteed."},{"t":104.72,"who":"nexubo","text":"Know its limits, and a stop-loss becomes a real plan, not a lucky charm."},{"t":116.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Both say BTC. But one may not give you any bitcoin at all. Next: spot vs futures."},{"t":123.15,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":18,"title":"Spot vs futures: you're not buying the same thing","goal":"Tell holding an asset apart from holding a price contract, and understand long and short.","task":"Match spot asset balances and futures position fields to the correct pages.","answer":"A futures position is not the same as withdrawable base-coin assets. The course doesn't require a real account.","takeaways":["Spot swaps one asset for another; a perpetual is a contract position.","Long gains if the price rises, short gains if it falls; the wrong side loses.","Futures are a different risk structure, not a level you must reach."],"quiz":{"q":"Does a futures position give you bitcoin you can withdraw?","options":["Yes, it is the same as spot","No, it is a contract that settles profit and loss","Only with 10x leverage"],"answer":1,"why":"A linear perpetual settles profit and loss in the contract. It is not a withdrawable coin balance."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"They both say bitcoin. Isn't it the same trade?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Plain spot, with no borrowing, swaps one asset for another. The linear perpetual we cover here is a contract position that settles profit and loss as the price moves. It's not buying bitcoin you can withdraw."},{"t":32.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Going long usually means hoping the price rises. Going short usually means hoping it falls. Get the direction wrong, and either side loses. Being able to short doesn't make it easier to make money."},{"t":60.25,"who":"sam","text":"Should I level up from spot to futures?"},{"t":62.38,"who":"nexubo","text":"No need. Futures are a different risk structure, not a level you must reach. You can understand them and still choose not to use them."},{"t":84.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Plain spot without borrowing has no margin liquidation like futures do. But the asset can still lose a lot of value. No liquidation doesn't mean your money is protected."},{"t":103.25,"who":"sam","text":"No."},{"t":103.9,"who":"nexubo","text":"Know what you're trading first. Then study leverage."},{"t":116.25,"who":"narrator","text":"10x leverage: does the exchange hand you 900 extra? Next episode."},{"t":121.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":19,"title":"10x leverage: more money, or more risk?","goal":"Tell margin, notional position and return on margin apart, and see what changing leverage does when position size is fixed.","task":"Choose 'fixed margin' and then 'fixed position' mode, and watch the money P&L after a price move.","answer":"With fixed margin, higher leverage enlarges the notional position. With a fixed position, money P&L doesn't grow automatically.","takeaways":["100 of margin controlling a 1,000 position is 10x leverage.","Price moves act on the position, and your margin takes the result.","With the same position size, changing leverage changes the margin, not the P&L."],"quiz":{"q":"A 1,000 position moves 1% against you, ignoring fees. About how much do you lose?","options":["1","10","100"],"answer":1,"why":"1% of the 1,000 position is about 10, taken from your margin."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"If I use ten times, does the exchange give me 900?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"It's not free cash. Simply put, 100 of margin controlling a 1,000 position is 10x leverage. Price moves act on the position, and your margin takes the profit or loss."},{"t":35.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Ignoring fees, a 1% move against you loses about 10 on a 1,000 position, and about 20 on a 2,000 position. The percentage looks small, but on a small margin it can hurt."},{"t":67.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Here's an easy mistake. If the position size stays the same and you only change the leverage setting, the P&L in money for the same price move doesn't double. What changes is the margin required and your risk buffer."},{"t":95.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Real liquidation also involves maintenance margin, the mark price and fees. You can't say 10x always liquidates at exactly a 10% drop. Don't use this shortcut for real trades."},{"t":116.25,"who":"sam","text":"No."},{"t":117.06,"who":"nexubo","text":"It magnifies how the position hits your money, not your judgement."},{"t":131.25,"who":"narrator","text":"You still have margin left. So why can you be forced out? Next: the line you can't see."},{"t":137.05,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":20,"title":"Why you can be closed out before your margin hits zero","goal":"Tell initial margin, maintenance margin, equity and available balance apart.","task":"Given initial margin 100, unrealised loss 20 and no fees, enter the simplified equity of 80, and spot which fields change.","answer":"Equity 80. The maintenance requirement is calculated by product rules and can't be assumed equal to the initial margin.","takeaways":["Initial margin opens a position; maintenance margin keeps it open.","Equity is margin plus or minus the open result: 100 down 20 leaves 80.","The risk system can step in before your margin reaches zero."],"quiz":{"q":"Initial margin is 100 and the position is down 20, ignoring fees. What is the simplified equity?","options":["100","80","120"],"answer":1,"why":"100 minus the 20 loss leaves 80, and the buffer keeps shrinking if the price moves further against you."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"There's still money there. Why won't it let me keep waiting?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"You need initial margin to open a position. While it's open, you must also meet the maintenance margin requirement. What you put in at the start isn't how much buffer you'll always have."},{"t":35.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Say the initial margin is 100 and the position is down 20. Ignoring everything else, the equity is now 80. If the price keeps moving against you, the buffer keeps shrinking."},{"t":67.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"When equity gets close to the platform's maintenance level, the risk system may step in. It doesn't have to wait for zero. It depends on the account type, margin mode and product rules."},{"t":95.25,"who":"sam","text":"So if I keep adding money, I'm safe?"},{"t":99.06,"who":"nexubo","text":"Adding money doesn't change the price direction, and it doesn't guarantee you won't be liquidated. It can pull more money into the same risk."},{"t":116.25,"who":"sam","text":"No. One is about opening the position. The other is about keeping it open."},{"t":121.2,"who":"nexubo","text":"Check equity first, then how far it is from the warning line."},{"t":131.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Put 100 in a box. Is the other 900 safe forever? Next: isolated margin."},{"t":137.95,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":21,"title":"Isolated margin: putting one trade in its own box","goal":"Understand per-position margin isolation, and how adding margin can increase the money at risk.","task":"Find the auto-add margin switch in the practice settings, and explain why it changes the money carrying the risk.","answer":"Automatic or manual adding increases the margin committed. You can't promise unconditionally to lose only the initial amount.","takeaways":["Isolated margin gives one position its own margin.","The margin you assigned can still be lost; isolated is not a stop-loss.","Auto-add or manual adding puts more of your money behind the same risk."],"quiz":{"q":"You turn on auto-add margin for an isolated position. What changes?","options":["Nothing, the loss stays capped at the first 100","More of your balance can flow into the position","The position becomes risk-free"],"answer":1,"why":"Auto-add moves available balance into the position, so more money carries the risk."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"If I put 100 in isolated, the other 900 can never be touched?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Isolated margin means a position gets its own margin. In a simple setup, with no adding and no auto-add, this position's liquidation risk is mainly carried by the margin you assigned."},{"t":32.25,"who":"narrator","text":"So it helps you separate the margin of different positions. But that 100 can still be lost. Isolated margin doesn't make losses disappear, and it isn't a stop-loss order."},{"t":60.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Watch this switch. Some platforms offer auto-add margin. Turn it on, and your available balance can keep flowing into the position. Adding margin by hand does the same. More money ends up carrying the risk."},{"t":84.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Don't see the word 'isolated' and assume the most you can lose is always that first 100. Check the add-margin settings, the fees and the product rules."},{"t":103.25,"who":"sam","text":"No."},{"t":103.99,"who":"nexubo","text":"With isolated margin, look at the walls, and also at any pipes plugged into the box."},{"t":116.25,"who":"narrator","text":"One position leaks, and another one starts sinking. Next: cross margin."},{"t":121.15,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":22,"title":"Cross margin: why one loss can hit your other positions","goal":"Understand the shared risk of one margin pool, without mistaking cross margin for all your personal assets.","task":"Let one virtual position lose, watch how risk changes across the same pool, and circle the assets that are not in this margin pool.","answer":"Risk can spread across the same margin pool. It doesn't automatically cover all your personal assets.","takeaways":["Cross margin shares one margin pool across positions.","One losing position can eat the buffer the others rely on.","It does not reach your bank cards or personal wallets."],"quiz":{"q":"Is cross margin automatically safer?","options":["Yes, it can never be liquidated","Not necessarily, it depends on the shared pool and the rules","Yes, it uses all your bank cards"],"answer":1,"why":"Extra margin can delay risk handling, but it also puts more of your money on the line."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"The loss is on the left one. Why is the right one in danger too?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Cross margin shares margin across positions, within what the rules allow. Here, say one margin pool holds 1,000. Several positions may depend on it, not just on what each was first assigned."},{"t":39.25,"who":"narrator","text":"One losing position can eat the buffer other positions rely on. Profits, losses, margin held for open orders, and the value of your collateral can all affect the same account's risk."},{"t":75.25,"who":"sam","text":"So cross margin is harder to liquidate, which makes it safer?"},{"t":79.67,"who":"nexubo","text":"Extra money can delay when one position hits risk handling. But it also puts more of your money on the line for the losses."},{"t":106.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Cross margin doesn't let a platform automatically use all your bank cards or personal wallets. Which accounts and collateral count depends on the product rules. Complex collateral can also come with haircuts and its own price risk."},{"t":130.25,"who":"sam","text":"Not necessarily. It depends on the shared pool and the account rules."},{"t":135.19,"who":"nexubo","text":"Look at the whole pool, not just one position's box."},{"t":146.25,"who":"narrator","text":"The price you watch hasn't hit the line, but the alarm is ringing. Next: who presses the liquidation button."},{"t":152.55,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":23,"title":"Liquidation: who pressed the forced-close button?","goal":"Understand liquidation triggers, the mark price and maintenance requirements, without using inverse leverage as an exact formula.","task":"Play the same virtual drop-then-rise market, and watch plain spot and a simplified isolated contract separately.","answer":"The path matters. A contract liquidated midway isn't restored by a later rebound.","takeaways":["Many perpetuals judge liquidation by the mark price and margin rules, not the last trade.","Near the maintenance requirement, the system can reduce or close the position.","A closed position does not come back when the price rebounds."],"quiz":{"q":"A leveraged position was liquidated, and later the price went back up. What happens?","options":["The position comes back on its own","It stays closed; the path mattered","The exchange refunds the loss"],"answer":1,"why":"Once force-closed, the position is gone. A later rebound only helps someone still holding."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"The price I'm watching hasn't got there. Why is the alarm going off?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"The last traded price isn't always the price used to judge liquidation. Many perpetuals assess risk using a mark price and margin rules. Check which rules your platform actually uses."},{"t":35.25,"who":"narrator","text":"When the price moves against the position, losses eat into equity. Near the maintenance requirement, the system may cancel orders, reduce the position or force-close it. It won't necessarily wait for your OK."},{"t":67.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Liquidation usually means this forced-close risk event, not your phone's button breaking. The real trigger depends on fees, maintenance margin, margin mode and more. 10x doesn't mean exactly a 10% drop."},{"t":95.25,"who":"sam","text":"It went back up later. Can I just wait for it to come back?"},{"t":99.46,"who":"nexubo","text":"A closed position doesn't come back on its own. A drop and then a rise can end very differently for a leveraged position and for plain spot you just held."},{"t":116.25,"who":"sam","text":"Not guaranteed."},{"t":117.73,"who":"nexubo","text":"It's not just the destination. It's whether you can survive the path."},{"t":131.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Price barely moved, and you didn't close, but your balance dropped. The final episode is next."},{"t":137.35,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]},{"ep":24,"title":"Price flat, balance down? Finish the last level with costs in mind","goal":"Know the direction and the base of funding fees, run a full futures risk check, and be able to choose not to trade.","task":"Calculate one period's fee of 0.10 and complete the virtual futures order checklist. Real order APIs must not be called.","answer":"Positive rate: longs pay, shorts receive. Fee in this example: 0.10. Pass by getting all key risk questions right, or by clearly choosing not to trade.","takeaways":["Funding is paid between longs and shorts, not a fixed charge to the exchange.","It is based on the position value: 1,000 at 0.01% is 0.10.","Choosing not to trade is a passing answer too."],"quiz":{"q":"The funding rate is positive. Who usually pays?","options":["Shorts pay longs","Longs pay shorts","Everyone pays the exchange"],"answer":1,"why":"With a positive rate, longs usually pay shorts. With a negative rate it flips."},"transcript":[{"t":0.25,"who":"sam","text":"The price barely moved and I didn't close. Why is my balance lower?"},{"t":9.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Besides trading fees, perpetual contracts usually have a funding mechanism. When the rate is positive, longs usually pay shorts. When it's negative, it flips. It's not a fixed interest everyone pays to the exchange."},{"t":42.25,"who":"narrator","text":"Say the position value at settlement is 1,000 and the rate is 0.01%. This fee is 0.10. It's based on the position value, not your first 100 of margin."},{"t":83.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"The interval and the rate can change by pair, platform and market. Don't remember it as 'every contract, every eight hours, forever'. And don't treat receiving funding as risk-free income."},{"t":117.25,"who":"narrator","text":"The last level is practice only. First explain what you're trading and which direction. Then check the mode, size, margin, auto-add, exit plan and costs. If you don't understand one item, pause. Don't guess your way through the buttons."},{"t":144.25,"who":"sam","text":"I know how to do it, and I know this risk isn't right for me. So I won't."},{"t":149.09,"who":"nexubo","text":"That's a passing result too. Finishing the course doesn't mean you can make money. Understand first, then act."},{"t":161.25,"who":"nexubo","text":"Whatever you trade, fees are a cost that adds up. Compare fees on NexuAlpha, and register through an official partner link with our code to get part of your fees back. Lower cost, same risk."},{"t":172.75,"who":"narrator","text":"You've finished both seasons. Pick the episode that felt hardest, and rewatch it before you ever trade."},{"t":178.65,"who":"narrator","text":"Understand first, then act."}]}]});
